[Music]  in 1631  a peculiar copy of the bible was  published it contained one  deeply blasphemous passage earning it  the name the wicked bible at first  glance the book seemed like any other  king james bible at the time  but if you were to turn to exodus 2014 a  passage where they expect to find the  commandment  thou shalt not commit adultery you'll  notice that has been changed  it now reads thou shalt commit adultery  the knot has been taken out so whilst  murder and theft are prohibited  cheating on your partner is apparently  fine  the wicked bible was not the work of  some secret adulterous sect within  christianity  nor did it reflect a lost version of the  original text  in fact it was the product of a simple  but crucial typo  when authorities discovered this blunder  multiple copies of this erroneous  bible had already been published and of  which there are actually 15 still around  today  oh and the publishers they lost their  book printing license swiftly after  the wicked bible is just one of many bad  copies of the good book  it's part of a collection of defective  bibles that have been named  bible errata or bible errors but i like  to call them  biblical bloopers so in this video  let's have a look at some of these  biblical bloopers and perhaps  some errors that have been left  unchanged  with the advent of the printing press in  the late middle ages it became far  quicker and cheaper to produce copies of  the bible one of the earliest bibles  ever produced by the printing press  was the gutenberg bible made sometime in  the 1450s  now previously monks had to  painstakingly copy out entire bibles by  hand  a process that took many years to  complete take for example the irish book  of kells  a beautifully decorated copy of the  gospels from around the year 800  and whilst its ornate pages preserve a  near perfect copy of the latin text  it notably gets a few verses wrong take  for example  matthew 10 34 now it should read  i came not to bring peace but the sword  but the new copy miswrites the latin  word for sword  gladium with joy gaudium  so the verse now becomes something like  i came not only to bring peace  but also joy it's an easy mistake to  make they look so similar when written  down  and plus the verse becomes a lot more  pleasant and certainly  less difficult to interpret  now printing bibles had the advantage  that there's no need to copy out things  by hand  it was a lot cheaper too and hopefully  fewer mistakes  right well as evidenced by the wicked  bible  nothing is quite promised in fact there  are plenty of misprinted bibles  other than the wicked bible so in what  follows  i'm going to prattle off some of the  best or worst depending on your  perspective  so many copies of the king james bible  contain the dreaded blasphemous comma  it occurs in luke 23 32  it describes how two criminals are  executed alongside jesus  in this passage the criminals are called  malefactors  an old-timey word for wrongdoers either  way  the passage should read and there were  also two others  malefactors led with him to be put to  death  yet in some copies the comma is removed  to produce  and there were also two other  malefactors led with him to be put to  death  lacking the appropriate comma implies  that jesus too was a criminal  who indeed deserved to be put to death  punctuation is important folks  much like the wicked bible the  unrighteous bible from 1653  gets rid of an important not before the  word inherit  in first corinthians 6 9 reading know ye  not that the unrighteous shall inherit  the kingdom of god  now this is quite a theologically  controversial message painting the  picture that the unrighteous were the  good guys and thus deserve to go to  heaven  similarly the fool's bible from 1763  amidst the word no at an important  moment  in psalm 14 1 it reads the fool has said  in his heart  there is a god this atheist typo set the  printers back  three thousand pounds  the discharge bible from 1806 replaces  the word  charge for discharge at a particularly  inappropriate moment  in first timothy 5 21. i discharge thee  before god  and the lord jesus christ and elect  angels that's disgusting  moving on my favorite has to be the owl  bible from 1944.  at one point in the book it replaces the  word own  for owl switching the n for the l so  in first peter 3 5 it reads  the holy women also who trusted god  adorn themselves  being in subjugation to their owl  husbands  at face value this biblical passage  implies the existence of a hybrid race  of owl people who apparently spend their  days subjugating human women with whom  they've married  now if anything i'd love to see an  apocryphal book expanding the law of  these mythical beasts  these printing errors are certainly  strange but miscopying the bible  or a biblical text is by no means a new  thing we've already seen how those monks  write in the book of kells were guilty  of god's slip up every now and then  but what about the mistakes that never  got fixed mistakes that have been  preserved in the final edition of the  bible  okay some context might be needed as  they are being written  the biblical texts were passed from hand  to hand and inscribed to scribe  and with each exchange bits were added  and other bits were taken away  now this can need a lot of room for  mistakes say a careless scribe wrote  something wrong  and no one checked up on it the mistake  may become part of the text  and passed down in future copies of the  bible  take for example jesus's name it's  actually well known that jesus wasn't  actually called  jesus but joshua we get the name jesus  from the greek yeasos  eases however is a greek version of the  hebrew name  yeshuva which is where we get our name  joshua  so that's why in the greek translation  of the old testament book of joshua  the main character is also called jesus  speaking of joshua i mean i mean jesus  the new testament jesus  you may have heard of one enigmatic  teaching that  it is easier for a camel to go through  the eye of a needle than for a rich man  to enter the kingdom of god  now this quote has long stumped  interpreters  why such a strange metaphor about camels  and needles  so some have thought that there's a gate  in jerusalem called the ardenidal  where camels would have to take off  their baggage to walk through  but unfortunately there remains little  to no archaeological evidence that this  ever was such a thing  in fact many now believe that all this  is a product of a typo  the first to suggest this was the early  church father cyril of alexandria  who claimed that camel is a greek  misspelling  and that camelos which is the greek word  for camel was written  in place of camilos meaning rope or  cable  so the message actually might have been  it is easier for a rope  to go through the eye of a needle than  for a rich man to enter the kingdom of  god  in my mind at least this seems to make a  lot more sense  it's often the case that translations of  the bible or any text for that matter  distort or warp the original meaning of  the text  just see how the word lucifer was  originally a translation of the planet  venus  or how the many biblical unicorns were  actually wild oxen  i actually have videos on both these  topics so feel free to give them a watch  after this  if these erroneous copies of the bible  reveal anything  it's that you can't trust everything you  read on paper  even if that paper is in the leaves of  the bible try as we might  we humans make mistakes and in the case  of misprinting a holy book  it can lead to controversial funny and  sometimes  outright blasphemous passages it really  gives new meaning to the phrase  the devils in the detail  hey thanks for watching i really hope  you enjoyed this video and welcome if  you're new to the channel  if you like this video and want to see  more why not subscribe  like and a comment also go a really long  way with my university terms starti